---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How to Set Up Your First Humidor (Step-by-Step) | Cigar Codex Guides"
  description="Seasoning, calibrating, and loading a new humidor for the first time — in order, with real target numbers."
>
  <div class="home-hero">
    <div class="cigar-hero__eyebrow">Buying Guide</div>
    <h1 class="home-hero__title">How to Set Up Your First Humidor (Step-by-Step)</h1>
  </div>

  <section class="section summary-review">
    <p>
      A new wood humidor doesn't hold humidity well until the wood itself is saturated — skipping
      this step is the most common reason a first humidor "doesn't work." Do these in order.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">1. Calibrate your hygrometer first</h2>
    <p>
      Before you do anything else, check whether your hygrometer is even accurate — see our
      <a href="/guides/best-hygrometers/">hygrometer guide</a> for the salt-test method. There's no
      point seasoning a humidor to a target number your gauge can't read correctly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">2. Season the wood (about two weeks)</h2>
    <p>
      Wipe down the interior cedar with distilled water on a clean cloth — damp, not soaked. Place
      a humidification source inside (a two-way pack is easiest for this step) and leave the
      humidor closed and empty, no cigars yet, for one to two weeks. Check daily and re-wipe the
      wood if it looks dry. This lets the cedar itself absorb enough moisture that it stops pulling
      humidity away from your cigars later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">3. Bring it to target RH</h2>
    <p>
      Once the wood is seasoned, get the box to 65-70% relative humidity before adding cigars.
      Compare Boveda-style packs, beads, and gel in our
      <a href="/guides/boveda-vs-beads-vs-gel/">humidification methods guide</a> if you haven't
      picked one yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">4. Load it — but not too full, and not too empty</h2>
    <p>
      A humidor holds humidity most evenly when it's reasonably full — an empty or nearly-empty box
      swings more than one that's stocked closer to capacity. If you're starting a collection, buy
      a humidor sized closer to what you'll actually own soon, not double it "to grow into."
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">5. Check weekly, then monthly</h2>
    <p>
      For the first month, check RH and refill/recharge your humidification source as needed —
      new setups drift more before settling in. After that, a monthly check is usually enough
      unless your hygrometer or humidity source flags a problem.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Related</h2>
    <p>
      Haven't bought a humidor yet? Start with
      <a href="/guides/best-humidors-for-beginners/">best humidors for beginners</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
